Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||05-03-2001||03:48 PM||||I noticed when I have the power windows down (and the sunroof removed) there seems to be no base on the factory radio (its the intermediate one, cassette with sliders for treble and base.<P>  sliding the base lever up and down does almost nothing! Close the windows and the low notes and the bass drum booms!<P> Is there that much accoustic differece with the windows up or down? Is it possible an amp or separate part of the stero is kicking offline - somehow crosswired with the power window circuits?<P>  Its like two completely different radios, almost as if someone had unplugged the big 6x9 speakers on the dash.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||stimpy||05-03-2001||04:26 PM||||I doubt there is any problem with your wiring. The bass is nothing but moving air. With the relatively small cabin, it's not too tough to make bass. When you open it up, you lose that small airspace.  <BR>Liken it to your a/c, which also works off of the same principal, moving air. With the windows closed, a properly functioning a/c in a Fiero will chill meat. Open the windows on a hot day, and your cooling efficiency is greatly diminished.<P>Of course, I recommend a good amp driving a 10" sub in a custom enclosure. Now, where could you get one of them? Hmmmmm....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||olliver||05-03-2001||04:48 PM||||the factory system was engineered to in volume of the cabin. Once the windows are opened, the volume substantialy increases, therefore, negating the effects of bass.  With an aftermarket system (ie 10" sub) you will find the exact opposite... with my windows down my bass acctually increases.<P>I own and run my own car audio installation shop here.  If anyone has any questions regarding car audio for the fiero or any car i would love to answer as best i can.  <BR>Thanx <BR>olliver||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hym-The-Man||05-03-2001||04:59 PM||||BASS is one of those weird things that takes awhile to explain.  basically what is happening is the bass waves are completing themselves outside of the car.  A typical bass wave takes more room than a car has to complete a cycle. For example: a friend of mine has a car that on a SPL(Sound Pressure Level) meter can only hit 128spl with the windows rolled up , but hits 158 with them down. This is why most competition vehicles are bigger, in general. <P>To compensate for the problem, you will have to add amplification to your system and or subwoofers.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||05-03-2001||05:16 PM||||All your "bass" are belong to us.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||12
Z||000005||Oreif||05-03-2001||06:34 PM||||If there are aftermarket speakers in the car, it is possible that the speakers are out of phase. This will cause the problem you are hearing.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||05-03-2001||08:28 PM||||Something has to be wrong. There shouldn't be a noticeable big difference on a stock Fiero system with windows up or down. Check wiring as oreif says. I once has a similar problem but was with incorrectly installed subs in firewall. With windows closed bass was gone. Now my system sounds almost identical with windows up or down. Also a good 8" sub will give you plenty of bass. Just put enough power. I have one in the stock location.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||05-04-2001||11:00 AM||||I tend to think something has to be wrong, cause with the windows down, I can slide the bass lever up and down and it has almost no effect, to the point where I almost thought it was disconnected internally.<P>  I think the speakers are from the factory, but I will check the phase of the connectors anyway.  In most cars the trunk acts like the resonant cavity for the rear deck speakers, and if you open the trunk you can hear the bass drop off.<P>  But the effect I am hearing is drastic. With the windows open the radio sounds like the old 7 transistor 9V battery Japanese Am radios I use to get when I was a kid, with the 2.5" speaker and the little thing that you put one end in your ear, and the other end wont go anywhere cause its bent.||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||05-04-2001||02:35 PM||||Hmmmm. How about when you adjust the bass level on the head unit? Are the dash & rear speakers affected too, or is it just the factory "subwoofer"?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||05-04-2001||03:49 PM||||there is a separate bass adjustment for the speakers in the headrest?!<P>where would the factory subwoofer be located?<P>the only thing that would make sense to me, the power windows do have circuits that cut power to the motor when the window is all the way down, so if an amp was mistakenly wired to the window 'down' power, it would be off when the windows are down.<P>  I will play with it somemore this weekend an see if I can make any sense out of it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||stimpy||05-04-2001||05:49 PM||||Oops. I misread. I was thinking that you were moving the slider for the Performance Sound. (You know if you had it, lil slider up by the maplights) On re-reading your posts, I figured that you were moving the bass adjust on the radio. <BR>Try moving fader to all front and all back, see if you can isolate the problem to one channel. ||||reg||1
